# Bad opens Q with no protection at all (11) -- fires.
# Good closes in finally (19) -- clean.
#
# Free / FreeAndNil / DisposeOf in a finally (38, 51, 63) are all CLEAN:
# TDataSet.Destroy closes before it frees, so no cursor leaks. The rule did not
# model this, and it was 95 of the findings on drag-lint's own source -- every
# one advising a Close the language already performs.
#
# 78 is the CONTROL that keeps the fix honest: a Free OUTSIDE any finally
# protects nothing on the exception path, so it must STILL fire. Without it,
# "recognise Free" and "switch the rule off" would look identical here.
dataset-open-without-close 11
!dataset-open-without-close 19
!dataset-open-without-close 38
!dataset-open-without-close 51
!dataset-open-without-close 63
dataset-open-without-close 78
#
# 101, 102, 104 (GoodOpenIsAField) are the 2026-09-17 FALSE POSITIVE: a record
# FIELD named Open, indexed with [ or passed to Length(). The walk used to match
# every exprDot spelt X.Open anywhere in the tree -- eight findings on
# drag-lint's own DRagLint.Doc.Facts.pas for one field. Only an X.Open that is
# the WHOLE statement (parent node statement, optionally with ()) is a
# dataset open; an operand of a subscript, an argument list or an assignment is
# not. 11 and 78 remain the positive controls that a bare Q.Open; still fires.
!dataset-open-without-close 101
!dataset-open-without-close 102
!dataset-open-without-close 104
